<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Claudication of both calves, worse on the right, in a 70-year-old touring singer with type 2 diabetes, coronary bypass surgery last year, and an ankle-brachial index of 0.62 right and 0.74 left. Please define the arterial disease, advise on exercise and antithrombotic therapy, and tell us whether and when revascularization should be considered, so that her limbs are protected before any tissue is lost. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within two to three weeks, on the same day as her two-week primary care visit. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Where is the arterial disease, and how severe is it?</item><item>Is supervised exercise therapy appropriate, and how should it fit around her touring?</item><item>Should antithrombotic therapy change, for example low-dose rivaroxaban with aspirin?</item><item>Is revascularization indicated now, and what would make it indicated later?</item><item>How often should her arteries and feet be reviewed, and what findings need urgent vascular review?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Fitzgerald, a 70-year-old singer, is seen at the request of Dr. Whitlow for calf pain on walking. For about a year both calves have cramped after one and a half blocks, the right sooner, easing within minutes of rest; she uses a wheelchair in large airports. She has no pain at rest or at night, no wounds, and no color change. She had five-vessel coronary bypass surgery last year and has no angina. She has diabetes with retinopathy and has never smoked. She has had no bleeding problems. She stands through long sets on stage and wants to keep touring. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Ella Jane Fitzgerald was born on April 25, 1917, in Newport News, Virginia, to Temperance and William Fitzgerald. Most biographies gave 1918, but her birth certificate and school records show 1917. [D]</item><item>In 1932 her mother died suddenly of a heart attack; orphaned at 15, she was placed in the Colored Orphan Asylum in Riverdale, New York. [D]</item><item>In 1934 she won an amateur contest at the Apollo Theater in Harlem, which brought her to the attention of the drummer and bandleader Chick Webb; she made her first recording with his band in 1935, and her 1938 swing version of the nursery rhyme &quot;A-Tisket, A-Tasket&quot; became her first hit. [D]</item><item>She won the Grammy Award for Best Jazz Individual Performance in 1958 for Ella Fitzgerald Sings the Duke Ellington Song Book, and received the Kennedy Center award for lifetime achievement in 1979. She was known as America&apos;s first lady of song. [D]</item><item>She began to have health problems during the 1970s; she had diabetes, which caused problems with her eyes. [D]</item><item>In the 1970s, after cataract surgery, she returned to touring more than 40 weeks a year. [D]</item><item>In 1986 she was admitted to intensive care for heart trouble and underwent quintuple-bypass heart surgery; the following year she was back onstage. [D]</item></list><paragraph>6 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

