<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Type 2 diabetes with peripheral artery disease (E11.51). Femoropopliteal peripheral artery disease in both legs: a right superficial femoral artery occlusion and a moderate left superficial femoral artery stenosis, with good flow to the feet. She has lifestyle-limiting claudication without signs of chronic limb-threatening ischemia (no rest pain, wound, or gangrene), so the limb is not at immediate risk. The first steps are supervised exercise therapy and medical therapy, which improve walking and lower the risk of heart attack, stroke, and limb events. Revascularization is considered when claudication still limits her life after a trial of exercise and medicines, and urgently if a wound, rest pain, or color change appears. Because amputation is more common in people with diabetes, and more common still, and more often done without a vascular assessment first, in Black Americans and in people with less access to care, she should have a low threshold for prompt vascular review of any foot problem. [S]</item><item>Coronary artery disease after bypass surgery (I25.10). Disease in two vascular beds. Low-dose rivaroxaban with aspirin lowers the risk of major cardiovascular and limb events in symptomatic peripheral artery disease, and her bleeding risk is low (no earlier bleeding, normal hemoglobin and kidney function, no anti-inflammatory painkillers). [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Whitlow&apos;s intake note and today&apos;s two-week note. Ankle-brachial index 0.62 right and 0.74 left; toe-brachial index 0.46 and 0.55. Creatinine 0.92 mg/dL, eGFR 67; hemoglobin 12.4 g/dL; LDL 92 mg/dL; A1c 8.6%. Arterial duplex in the vascular laboratory today: aortoiliac and common femoral arteries without significant narrowing; right superficial femoral artery occluded for about 9 cm in its middle third, with flow restored in the above-knee popliteal artery and two open tibial arteries to the foot; left superficial femoral artery narrowed by 50 to 69% with an open popliteal artery and three tibial arteries; calcified tibial arteries on both sides. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

