    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>No revascularization now. [S]</item><item>Supervised exercise therapy: treadmill walking to moderate calf pain, 30 to 45 minutes three times a week for twelve weeks, with a structured home walking plan for tour weeks. [S]</item><item>Start rivaroxaban 2.5 mg twice daily with aspirin 81 mg daily; avoid anti-inflammatory painkillers. [S]</item><item>Continue atorvastatin 80 mg and add ezetimibe if LDL stays at 55 mg/dL or above; a GLP-1 receptor agonist such as semaglutide, which increased walking distance in people with peripheral artery disease and type 2 diabetes, is worth considering when her glucose plan is reviewed. [S]</item><item>Reassess after twelve weeks of exercise; if claudication still limits her life, consider endovascular treatment of the right superficial femoral artery. [S]</item><item>Urgent vascular review, the same or next day, for rest pain, a foot wound that does not heal within two weeks, or a cold, pale, or dark toe. [S]</item><item>Vascular review with ankle-brachial index and duplex in six months, and yearly after that if stable. [S]</item><item>High-risk foot program with podiatry every three months and protective, low-heeled shoes on and off stage. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Whitlow, who saw her the same day. The findings and choices were explained to Ms. Fitzgerald, who chose to begin supervised exercise and rivaroxaban; she decides who else receives this note. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Message review with Dr. Whitlow after twelve weeks of supervised exercise; vascular clinic in six months with ankle-brachial index and duplex; sooner for any of the urgent signs. [S]</item></list></text>

